<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Egba United Government</h1><p class="st-meta">1830 CE – 1914 CE (approx.) · capital Abeokuta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Egba state of Abeokuta, a Yoruba refugee city that became a centre of missions and returned Saro, and stayed formally independent of Britain until 1914.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Egba lost their towns in the wars that followed the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. Around 1830 survivors under the war leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sodeke">Sodeke</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olumo-rock">Olumo</a> rock. The new city united many Egba townships, each keeping its own chiefs, and in 1854 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okukenu">Okukenu</a> was installed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland">Alake</a>, paramount ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Returned freed slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, known as Saro, and missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-townsend">Henry Townsend</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Western education. The Egba twice repelled sieges by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a>, in 1851 and 1864. In 1865 the Saro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-johnson">George William Johnson</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egba-united-board-of-management">Egba United Board of Management</a>, and in 1898 a reformed Egba United Government ran a treasury, courts and roads under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland">Alake</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gbadebo-i">Gbadebo I</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A treaty of 1893 recognised Egba independence, but British influence grew. After unrest in 1914 the Egba government lost its independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland">Alake</a> remained a leading traditional ruler.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/egba-united-government.jpg" alt="Egba United Government" width="320" height="429"><figcaption>Image: OmoIyaLeke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigeria_benin_kingdoms_Egba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1830 CE – 1914 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abeokuta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sodeke, Okukenu, Gbadebo I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yoruba religion, Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Egbaland, Egba, Abeokuta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=egba-united-government" aria-label="Egba United Government on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4773 1910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Egba United Government lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5093 2166L5109 2141M5093 2166L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5109 2141h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5093 2166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5093 2166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Abeokuta. <a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=egba-united-government">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
